  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "### Minor Changes\n\n- Licenses that cover only some versions of your app, such as \"12 months of updates\" or \"versions up to 2.x\", are now supported. `License.ReleaseAccess` says what a license covers and `License.CoversVersion` checks a version against it. When a license doesn't cover `LicensingOptions.ApplicationVersion`, activating or validating it throws the new `ReleaseNotCoveredException`, with what the license covers and, from Moonbase, an `UpgradeUrl` to offer. `LicenseValidator.ValidateLicense` now refuses such a version on the device too, offline licenses included; set `EnforceReleaseAccess` to false to check it yourself. The exception derives from `LicenseInvalidException`, so if your app deletes the stored license on that, catch `ReleaseNotCoveredException` first and keep the license, which still covers older versions. ([#378](https://github.com/Manevann/moonbase/pull/378))\n- Add-ons that the merchant includes in the main product's license now come with the license: `License.SubProducts` lists each one the customer owns or trials, with whether it may be unlocked on this device, and `License.HasSubProduct(\"addon\")` tells you whether to unlock it, checking its expiry too. Start an add-on's trial from the main license with `ILicenseClient.RequestSubProductTrial(license, \"addon\")`, or take a seat of it with `ILicenseClient.ActivateSubProduct(license, \"addon\")`, and store the license either returns. An add-on whose seat the customer revoked on this device reads `Revoked` until the app activates it again, and `LicensingOptions.AutoActivateSubProducts = false` turns off taking add-on seats on activation and validation. `ILicenseClient.ListSubProducts(license)` lists the add-ons the customer can act on, for an in-app store or upgrade screen, each with its prices, whether the customer owns it, whether to unlock it, whether a trial can be started, and a buy link. See \"Add-ons\" in the README. `ILicenseClient` gains these three methods, so a class of your own implementing it needs them too. ([#375](https://github.com/Manevann/moonbase/pull/375))\n\n  Fixed: custom properties (`License.Properties`, `Product.Properties` and `IssuedTo.Properties`) were always null in apps on the IdentityModel version this package depends on. They are now read whichever version the app ends up with.",
